 "derivation": "Two of the four are genuinely Avestan, two are back-projections. (i) 'â-kand, â-gand \"to fill\"': Avestan has no ā-kan-; the compound that means 'to fill up (a pit) by digging' is aipi-kan- (AiW col. 437: 'mit aipi \"durch Graben ausfüllen, zuschütten\": … aŋhā zəmō aipi-kanyāṯ V.14.6'), and the ā- prefixed 'fill' verb is first attested in Parthian ʾgn-/āgn- 'to fill' and MP āgand (Cheung, *kan² 'to fill', p.232). So the form is right in substance (Old Iranian *ā-kan-ta-) but the language label 'Avestan' is an extrapolation. (ii) 'aiwi-kand \"to throw away\"' — this is Nourai's prefix for NP afkandan; but MP abgandan and NP afgandan go back to *apa-kan-/*upa-kan- (Nyberg: '< OIr *apa-kan- and *upa-kan-, from kandan'; Cheung: '(+ *apa-) NP afgandan \"to throw, cast away\"'), so the prefix is apa-, not aiβi-/*ambhi-. (iii) 'para-kan \"spread around\"' is right in kind — Bartholomae has the para- compound and Cheung derives NP parākandan from *para-ā-kan-, though under the throw-root *kan¹, not under 'dig'. (iv) 'paiti-kan \"front digger, arrow\"' is the classical etymology of peykān: Horn no. 362 (p.80) 'peikān \"Pfeilspitze, Pfeil\". aw. paiti + √kan- (?)' — with Horn's own question mark; Cheung lists NP paykandan '(+ *pati-)' likewise with a query.",
