 "derivation": "All five Persian words are real and all five continue an Old Iranian kan- verb, exactly as drawn. آگندن/آکندن āgandan 'to fill, stuff' < MP āgandan, āgan- (MacKenzie: 'āgandan, āgan- [ʾkn-dn' | M ʾgnd, N -] fill, stuff'), < OIr *ā-kan-ta-; Abolghasemi (FFD p.26) spells the chain out: 'آگند < فارسی میانه āgand < ایرانی باستان *ā-kan-ta-'. کژاکند kažākand / قزاگند qazāgand is the padded battle-coat, a compound of kaž 'raw silk' + āgand 'stuffed' — Borhān-e Qāṭeʿ (vol.3, pdf p.321 ≈ printed 1526-1528) defines قزاغند as 'جامه‌ای که در حشو آن پنبه و ابریشم آکنده و آجیده کرده باشند' and Moʿin's footnote equates قزاغند = قزاکند = کژاکند = کژاغند. افکندن/افگندن afkandan 'to throw, drop' < MP abgandan, abgan- < *apa-kan-/*upa-kan-. پراکندن/پراگندن parākandan 'to scatter' < MP pargandan/parāgandan < *para-ā-kan- (Parthian prʾgn-, MP pargandag 'scattered'). پیکان peykān 'arrowhead' < MP paykān < *pati-kāna- 'that which is driven in' (Horn 362), with the regular pati- > pay- of Middle/New Persian. The one caveat is the one already noted at the root: Abolghasemi, on the very page Nourai cites, warns that the kan- behind āgandan/afgandan/parāgandan is NOT the kan- behind kandan 'to dig'.",
